# Ownership for the files that decide what CI proves and what the project ships.
#
# This routes review rather than blocking merges: the branch rules require a
# pull request but not an approval, so a maintainer is requested here
# automatically and the change lands in a queue rather than sitting unnoticed.
#
# The security argument for these paths specifically: a pull request runs the
# workflow files *as they exist in that pull request*. A change that deletes the
# zizmor job, or the licence and advisory gate, disables the very check that
# would have objected to it. Requiring an owner here is what closes that loop.
#
# No `*` catch-all. It would name an owner for every path in the repository and
# make the specific entries below look like they carry no more weight than the
# rest, when the point of the list is that they carry more.

# CI definitions, the composite action, and the changed-path classifier that
# decides which jobs run at all.
/.github/               @MarcusKainth
/scripts/               @MarcusKainth

# Supply-chain surface: the licence/advisory/bans policy, the resolved graph
# every gate is evaluated against, and the generated attribution inventory.
#
# `/about/` belongs here as much as `about.toml` does. The templates decide what
# the published attribution *says* — the terms we claim to comply with, and the
# caveats a reader relies on — so editing one is a compliance change wearing the
# clothes of a formatting change.
/deny.toml              @MarcusKainth
/about.toml             @MarcusKainth
/about/                 @MarcusKainth
/Cargo.lock             @MarcusKainth
/THIRD-PARTY.md         @MarcusKainth

# Release surface: what gets published, under which version, and by which
# credential. A change here can publish something, so it belongs on this list
# for the same reason the workflows do.
/release-plz.toml       @MarcusKainth
/RELEASING.md           @MarcusKainth

# Published security claims. These have been wrong before — the nightly they
# described was disabled for eighteen days — so a change here should be a
# deliberate one.
/SECURITY.md            @MarcusKainth
/docs/user-guide/07-reference/supply-chain.mdx  @MarcusKainth
